Sumi, the owner's text editor, the first of the owner's programs on Zenix (roadmap block 3: the text-UI library,
then Sumi, Maki, Markdown; DESIGN D27 section 9; docs/d27-c-library.md, "Sumi on Zenix"; the user's page is
docs/man/sumi.txt)

What it is: a text editor in the way of micro and nano that draws in the terminal (tabs, colours for code, find and
replace, undo, macros), written for AmigaOS over tui.library. On Zenix it is /bin/sumi, a program with shared code
(ZEX2: a second Sumi costs only its data), linked with the text-UI library's pure build (build/libtui-pure.a, ports/tui)
and the C library's (build/libc-pure.a).

upstream/  the owner's source, one source for AmigaOS and Zenix (D27 9, rule 1; owner 2026-10-28), pinned here and
           never changed: the owner's TextTools repository (public, MIT), commit
           533439214cf4225feed4f0e748c3b3f20099e60b (2026-10-22), Sumi 0.2b6: src/*.c and src/sumi.h, README.md as README
           (the user's documentation, which docs/man/sumi.txt follows) and LICENSE (MIT, the notice that travels with the
           program: tools/mkroot.sh and the package sumi put it in /usr/share/licenses/sumi, with tui's).
zenix/     Zenix's layer (GPL-2.0-only), kept apart so the copy can be refreshed. Upstream calls AmigaOS directly (it
           has no OS layer of its own), so the layer is AmigaOS's names over the C library:
           include/   amigados.h: the types, the structures upstream reads (FileInfoBlock, DateStamp, the clipboard's
                      request) and the calls, each one's meaning on Zenix in its head; the exec/, dos/, devices/,
                      intuition/ and proto/ headers upstream includes are amigados.h (and so are those the text-UI
                      library's headers include, in this build)
           dos.c      the calls: Open/Read/Write/Seek/Close/FGets (a handle is the descriptor + 1, so 0 stays
                      "failed"), Lock/UnLock/Examine/ExNext/SameLock/CreateDir (a lock is the path, its device and
                      inode), DeleteFile, Rename, SetProtection (the Unix mode, and root's owner), IoErr and Fault
                      (errno and strerror), CompareDates, SystemTags (system()), FindTask, the clipboard device
                      (never opens)

Decided here (conservatively; for the main session to confirm):
- The names: only three of upstream's own literals are mapped, nothing else is ever rewritten (a file the user names
  is a Unix path as typed): "ENVARC:Sumi" is $HOME/.config/Sumi (made at the first start, mode 700: Sumi.places lists
  the files edited), "ENV:Sumi" is no file (AmigaOS keeps a RAM copy of the settings there; on Zenix one place is
  enough, so that copy is never written and never read), "T:Sumi-" is /tmp/Sumi- (a file never named, kept when the
  terminal goes away). No HOME: no settings are kept.
- Protection bits: FileInfoBlock's protection carries the Unix mode (bits 8-19, bit 31 marking it) and FIBF_WRITE
  when this user may not write the file (upstream then shows [read-only] and refuses to save over it, as for an
  AmigaOS file protected against writing). FIBF_DELETE is never set: a directory that cannot be written fails the
  save with its own message. SetProtection puts the mode back after the save (a new copy renamed over the old file)
  and, when root saves, the owner and group Examine saw, so root editing a user's file leaves it the user's.
- Comments: Zenix files have none; Examine gives an empty one, so upstream never sets one.
- The clipboard: none shared between programs on Zenix yet; clipboard.device never opens and upstream keeps the
  copied text itself (shared by its tabs, gone when it ends).
- "run COMMAND": through sh (system()), in the terminal, as upstream runs an AmigaDOS command.
- The stack: upstream swaps to a 32 KB stack of its own when the Shell gave less; on Zenix the program's stack is the
  loader's (D27 9 rule 5) and FindTask answers a task with enough, so upstream never swaps. STACK_DEFAULT (16 KB):
  -fstack-usage gives under 1.2 KB per frame (file_save the largest), the deepest chain a few KB; the one recursion,
  the regular expressions' re_here (60 bytes a level), is at most one level per repeat in a 128-byte pattern.

Found in upstream (for the owner's repository, not changed here):
- Saving goes through a new copy renamed over the file (file.c): through a soft link (AmigaOS's too) the link is
  replaced by a file and its target keeps the old text; resolving the link first would keep it.
- Memory: about 194 KB of data while one file is open on 80 x 32 (ps), of which the text-UI library's two grids are
  80 KB (16-byte cells) and the places table 26 KB, allocated whole at the first file (100 entries of 264 bytes:
  PATHMAX 256); a smaller cell, a growing table or a shorter PATHMAX would each matter on the stock A1200.
- Messages and help keep AmigaOS words: "Protect it +wd first", "an AmigaDOS command", "ENVARC:Sumi/Sumi.keys" in
  "does nothing"; on Zenix they mean chmod, a shell command and $HOME/.config/Sumi. A word from the OS layer
  (upstream's own small header) would make them right on both.
- Shift+Tab (unindent) needs the terminal to send ESC [ Z; Zenix's console sent a plain Tab for it (a console matter,
  not upstream's): fixed in the keyboard (kernel/kbd.c, 2026-10-29), checked by tools/sumi-test.sh.

Tests: tools/sumi-test.sh (FS-UAE, the stock A1200; in make check): sumi opens a file, the grid is read back with
termtest screen (CON_READ), keys are put in (CON_INPUT), the saved file is compared byte for byte, the settings and
the cursor's place checked at a second start, the memory printed.

To update: tools/sumi-vendor.sh /path/to/TextTools [COMMIT] (the files above from that commit, unchanged), put the
commit here, build, run tools/sumi-test.sh, and bring docs/man/sumi.txt in step with the README's changes; if
upstream starts to use an AmigaOS call amigados.h does not have, the build says so (an undeclared function).
